 "traditions": [],
 "notes": "Bridle: old flat headstall SKUs folded into archetypes (Western headstall versions) or kept as ≤6 models; bridle names (snaffle, double, pelham, Barcoo, halter, driving) became archetypes; gag bridle is a type. Nosebands are versions of the snaffle bridle (sourced from Bridle/Noseband pages). Snaffle bit: cheek types are archetypes, mouthpieces are types; Waterford kept as type. Curb bit: Weymouth, pelham, kimblewick, western curb, spade, Liverpool as archetypes; Chifney (an in-hand anti-rearing bit) kept as one model. Martingale kind kept tier-style with martingale and breastplate types, as briefed. Left out: Myler and Neue Schule product archetypes (no retrieved product page; Myler page 404), Stübben (brand only, from knowledge), Micklem award year, Neue Schule country/founding (not on a retrieved page). Old 'Horse reins' renamed 'Reins'. Weaver Equine maker names normalised to Weaver Leather; Herm. Sprenger Metallwarenfabrik shortened to Herm. Sprenger."
}